<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sixty Dome Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Shait Gambuj Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sixty Dome Mosque is a 15th-century brick mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagerhat">Bagerhat</a> in south-western Bangladesh, attributed to the frontier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> Khan Jahan Ali during the Bengal Sultanate. Despite its name its prayer hall has 77 domes, carried on rows of stone pillars, with corner towers. It is the largest historic mosque in Bangladesh and the centre of the Bagerhat World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sixty-dome-mosque.jpg" alt="Sixty Dome Mosque" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: মোয়ায মাহমুদ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixty_Dome_Mosque,Bagerhat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
